If $k \not\in\{1,j\}$, 
similar reasoning produces a 4-cycle with labels obtained by adding $\vec{e}^{\,d}_k$ to each label in the cycle on the left. Inductively, we can add an arbitrary sum 
 $\vec{v}=\sum_{k \not\in\{1,j\}} \vec{e}^{\,d}_k$ to each label,  using
the diagram on the right as a guide.  This yields 4-cycles based on  $\vec{e}^{\,d}_1$ and
 $\vec{e}^{\,d}_j$ covering all of $Q^0_d$ and
exhibiting the rotation $ P_1 P_{1j}$ explicitly.

Let $H$ be a rigid $d$-cube in $G$ with a grid neighborhood  $N_G(H)$,
identified with a neighborhood of the unit 
cube $Q^0_d = \{0,1\}^{\,d}$ in $\mathbb{Z}^d$. To reduce notational confusion, we denote elements of $N_G(H)$
by $h_{S^*} = \sum _{ k \in S^*} \pm  \vec{e}^{\,d} _{|k|}$, $S^* \subseteq \{\pm 1,\dots,\pm d\}$.
For example, $h_{-1} = - \vec{e}^{\,d}_1 = - h_1$ and $h_\emptyset = \vec{0}^d$. 
 As before, $Q_{d+1}$ is identified with the unit cube $Q^0_{d+1}=\{0,1\}^{d+1}$ in $\mathbb{Z}^{d+1}$,
 with vertices $\vec{e}^{\,d+1}_S$, $S \subseteq \{1,\dots,d+1\}$.  

 We will repeatedly use the fact that, in a grid graph of any dimension, 
 two distinct vertices $\vec{u}$ and $\vec{v}$ can have at most two common neighbors. 

Let $\sigma: Q^0_{d+1} \to G$ be a singular $(d+1)$-cube in which $f^-_{d+1}\sigma$ is supported by $H$. We will show that $\sigma$ has at 
least one more face supported by $H$. Suppose, to the contrary, that there is no such face. In particular, $f^+_{d+1}\sigma$ is not supported by $H$, so some element in $ H$ does not appear in $\mathrm{im}(f^+_{d+1}\sigma)$.   
Assume, without loss of generality, that 
$h_\emptyset\in H$ is such an element, with $\sigma(\vec{0}^{d+1}) = h_\emptyset= \vec{0}^d$ and also
$\sigma(\vec{e}^{\,_{d+1}}_{i}) = h_i$ for $i = 1,\dots,d$. Note that, under this assumption, $h_\emptyset$ appears only once in $\mathrm{im}(\sigma)$.

We first show that, if  $x= \sigma(\vec{e}^{\,_{d+1}}_{d+1})$, then  $x \in \{h_1,\dots,h_d\}$. Otherwise
$x$ would be another vertex in $N_G(H)$ adjacent to $h_\emptyset$, i.e.,  $x  \in \{-h_2,\dots,-h_d\}$.  But if 
$x = -  h_i$, then $\sigma(\vec{e_i} + \vec{e_{d+1}}) =h_\emptyset$ is forced, since $h_\emptyset$ is the only common neighbor of $h_i$ and $-h_i$ in $N_G(H)$:
