        \Edge[style={thick},color=black](1)(3)
          \Edge[style={thick},color=black](2)(4)      
          \end{tikzpicture} 
    \end{center}
This contradicts the assumption that $h_\emptyset$ occurs only once in $\sigma$. 
Thus $\sigma(\vec{e}^{\,_{d+1}}_{d+1})= h_k$, for some $k \in \{1,\dots,d\}$. 
Using this value of $k$, we will prove that
 that $f_k^-(\sigma)$ is a second face supported by $H$.

The argument will show (by induction on $|S|$) that for all subsets
$S \subseteq \{1,2,\dots, \hat{k}, \dots,d\}$, we have 
\begin{equation}\label{remainingterms}
\sigma( \vec{e}^{\,d+1}_S+ \vec{e}^{\,d+1}_{k})=\sigma( \vec{e}^{\,d+1}_S+ \vec{e}^{\,d+1}_{d+1})= h_{S\cup k}. 
\end{equation}
Note that
the values of $\sigma( \vec{e}^{\,d+1}_S)$ range over the common face of $f^-_{d+1}\sigma$ and $f^-_{k}\sigma$; 
in (\ref{remainingterms}) we are showing that the remaining values on those  two faces agree.  
