The vertex $v$ must appear somewhere on the top face of $\sigma$, since otherwise $\sigma$ is a singular cube for the ordinary grid graph
$I_8^3$, contradicting Theorem \ref{maintheorem}. Furthermore, $v$ is linked only to vertices $\vec{0}$ and $\vec{1}$ in $G$, and thus
can appear only at one (or both) of the positions corresponding to $0$ and $7$ on the bottom face. If it appears only once, then $\sigma$ is 
a singular cube for a subgraph of $I_8^3$, again contradicting Theorem \ref{maintheorem}. If it appears twice, then the path
of length $3$  between the two occurrences of $v$ in in $\sigma$ induces a cycle of length $\le3$ containing $v$ in $K^{\{3\}}$.  But 
the smallest cycle in $K^{\{3\}}$ containing $v$ has length $5$, a contradiction.  This argument generalizes easily to dimensions $d>3$.

