To obtain a monotolerant witness for $K_{23}^{\{d\}}$, $d>3$, construct a singular $d+1$ cube $\sigma$ as follows. Let $f_{d+1}^{-}(\sigma)$ be a $d$-cube with vertices
labeled by $0,\dots,2^d-1$ as above. To label upper face $f_{d+1}^{+}(\sigma)$, first assign label $1$ to the vertex opposite $0$; then assign label $v$ to all of the vertices opposite $1, 2, 4, \dots, 2^{d-1}$.
Finally, for each remaining vertex labeled $k$ in $f_{d+1}^{-}(\sigma)$, write $k$ in binary notation, drop the last two binary digits, and assign that label to the vertex opposite $k$. This pattern 
produces  a singular cube $\sigma$ for  $K_{23}^{\{d\}}$ and every face except $f_{d+1}^{-}(\sigma)$ contains a vertex labeled $v$.

{\rm (Sketch)}
Consider the dual graph $G^*_K$ of $G_K$, whose vertices are the 2-cells of $K$ and whose edges are adjacent pairs of 2-cells. This graph partitions $S^2$ into regions corresponding to the original 0-cells of $K$, in which each vertex has degree 4.  Elementary graph theory shows that we may 
cover the edges of $G^*_K$ by a collection of disjoint directed cycles in which edges entering a vertex always leave by the opposite edge (a local condition). Now associate to every vertex $z \in V(G^*_K)$ a singular cube $\tilde{z}$ according to the following rule,  which picks a  vertex to ``anchor'' the cube among four possible orientation-preserving rotations.
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth,scale=.6]
\draw[->, very thick] (0,.3) -> (0,2) node[above]{};
\draw[->,very  thick] (.3,0) -> (2,0) node[right]{};
\draw[->,very  thick] (-2,0) -> (-.3,0) node[right]{};
\draw[->, very thick] (0,-2) -> (0,-.3) node[right]{};

\node [rectangle, draw] at (0,0) {$z$};
\node at (1,1) {\color{red}$a$};
\node at (-1,1) {\color{red}$b$};
\node at (-1,-1) {\color{red}$c$};
\node at (1,-1) {\color{red}$d$};
\node at (3.2,0) {$\Longrightarrow $};
\draw (4.5,-1) rectangle (6.5,1);
\draw (4.5,0) -- (6.5,0);
\draw (5.5,-1) -- (5.5,1);
\node at (6,-.5) {\color{red}$a$};
\node at (6,.5) {\color{red}$b$};
\node at (5,.5) {\color{red}$c$};
\node at (5,-.5) {\color{red}$d$};

\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}
This rule associates every cell in $K$ to a singular $2$-cube, and it is straightforward to check,
examining all possibilities, that boundary edges of adjacent cells always cancel (they have the same orientation but 
opposite sign).
 Hence  $\tilde{Z} = \sum_{i=1}^m \alpha_i\tilde{z}_i$ is a cycle in $C_2(G_k)$, as claimed.
